Let \(M_1\) be an NMOS biased in saturation with small‑signal parameters \(g_m,\; r_o\).  In small signal, \(V_{DD}\) is AC ground and the MOS gate draws negligible current.

## 1) Feedback factor
The divider senses \(v_{out}\) and feeds the gate:
\[
v_g=v_F=\beta v_{out},\qquad 
\beta=\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}
\]
Also, from the output node to ground the divider looks like a load \(R_1+R_2\).

## 2) Small‑signal gain \(A_v=v_{out}/v_{in}\)
With \(v_s=v_{in}\), \(v_d=v_{out}\):
\[
i_d = g_m(v_g-v_s)+\frac{v_d-v_s}{r_o}
= g_m(\beta v_{out}-v_{in})+\frac{v_{out}-v_{in}}{r_o}
\]
KCL at the output node gives
\[
\frac{v_{out}}{R_D}+\frac{v_{out}}{R_1+R_2}+i_d=0
\]
Solving for \(A_v\):
\[
\boxed{\;
\frac{v_{out}}{v_{in}}
=
\frac{g_m+\frac{1}{r_o}}
{\frac{1}{R_D}+\frac{1}{R_1+R_2}+\frac{1}{r_o}+g_m\beta}
\;}
\]

If \(r_o\to\infty\):
\[
\boxed{\;
A_v \approx
\frac{g_m}
{\frac{1}{R_D}+\frac{1}{R_1+R_2}+g_m\beta}
\;}
\]

### High loop‑gain limit (key insight)
If \(g_m\beta \gg \frac{1}{R_D}+\frac{1}{R_1+R_2}+\frac{1}{r_o}\), then
\[
A_v \approx \frac{1}{\beta}
= \frac{R_1+R_2}{R_2}
= 1+\frac{R_1}{R_2}
\]
So the circuit behaves like a **non‑inverting amplifier** whose gain is set mainly by the resistor ratio.

## (Optional) Input and output resistances
Let \(G_D=1/R_D,\;G_L=1/(R_1+R_2),\;g_{ds}=1/r_o\).

- Output resistance (set \(v_{in}=0\), apply test at output):
\[
\boxed{\;
R_{out}=
\frac{1}{G_D+G_L+g_{ds}+g_m\beta}
\;}
\]
(feedback lowers \(R_{out}\) by adding the term \(g_m\beta\)).

- Input resistance (current into the source terminal), using the solved \(A_v\):
\[
\boxed{\;
R_{in}=
\frac{G_D+G_L+g_{ds}+g_m\beta}{(g_m+g_{ds})(G_D+G_L)}
\;}
\]
(series mixing raises \(R_{in}\) versus a plain common‑gate stage).

If you want, I can also do the **DC bias** equations (how \(V_{out}\) and \(V_F\) self‑set given \(V_{in,DC}\), \(R_D\), and the MOS \(I\!-\!V\) law).

To find \(Z_\text{out}\), set the independent sources to AC ground: \(V_{DD}\to 0\), \(V_b\to 0\), and \(v_{in}\to 0\). Then the gate of \(M_1\) is tied to ground **through \(R_S\)**, not an ideal AC ground.

Now apply a small test voltage \(v_x\) at \(v_\text{out}\) (the **source** of \(M_1\)). Because \(M_1\) has a gate–source capacitance \(C_{gs}\), changes in \(v_\text{out}\) drive displacement current through \(C_{gs}\) into the gate node. Since the gate can only return to ground through \(R_S\), the gate voltage moves with a frequency-dependent phase shift:

\[
\frac{v_g}{R_S}+sC_{gs}(v_g-v_x)=0
\;\;\Rightarrow\;\;
v_{gs}=v_g-v_x=-\frac{v_x}{1+sR_SC_{gs}}
\]

That \(v_{gs}\) produces a transconductance current \(g_m v_{gs}\) and there is also the capacitor current through \(C_{gs}\). The resulting small-signal output admittance (ignoring \(r_o\) for clarity) is

\[
Y_\text{out}\approx \frac{g_m+sC_{gs}}{1+sR_SC_{gs}}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
Z_\text{out}\approx \frac{1+sR_SC_{gs}}{g_m+sC_{gs}}
\]

In the frequency range where \(sC_{gs}\ll g_m\) (i.e., \(\omega \ll g_m/C_{gs}\)) this becomes

\[
Z_\text{out}\approx \frac{1+sR_SC_{gs}}{g_m}
= \frac{1}{g_m} + s\frac{R_SC_{gs}}{g_m}
\]

The term proportional to \(s=j\omega\) is exactly the impedance of an inductor. So, over that range,

\[
Z_\text{out}\;\approx\;\underbrace{\frac{1}{g_m}}_{\text{series }R}
\;+\;\underbrace{j\omega\left(\frac{R_SC_{gs}}{g_m}\right)}_{\text{series }L}
\]

with an effective inductance

\[
L_\text{eq}\approx \frac{R_S C_{gs}}{g_m}
\]

So the output can look **inductive** because \(R_S\) together with \(C_{gs}\) creates a delayed (phase-shifted) gate motion, and \(g_m\) converts that into a current whose phase corresponds to an inductor. (Including \(r_o\) of \(M_1\) and \(M_2\) just adds parallel resistive components; it doesn’t remove the inductive term.)

Assuming small-signal analysis with \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) as AC ground (ideal bias sources), this is a **PMOS cascode stack** and the resistance seen at the bottom node is:

\[
R_{\text{out}}
= \frac{g_{m1}+g_{ds1}+g_{ds2}}{g_{ds1}g_{ds2}}
= r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
\]

So the usual high-gain approximation is:

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}} \approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\quad \text{if } g_{m1}r_{o1},\, g_{m1}r_{o2} \gg 1
\]

(If body effect is included for \(M_1\), replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\).)

Yes — **if you mean the resistance of the running oscillator**, the answer generally changes, and the simple expression is no longer the right model.

The expression

\[
R_X \simeq \frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{1}{g_{mn}}+\frac{1}{g_{mp}}\right)
\]

is a **small-signal result around the symmetric dc operating point** where all inverter inputs and outputs sit near the switching point. In that special bias condition, by symmetry, each inverter’s input and output move together for a supply perturbation, so each inverter looks roughly like an NMOS and PMOS diode-connected in series. Three identical inverters in parallel gives the factor \(1/3\).

But that operating point is the **metastable/startup point** of the ring oscillator. Once the oscillator is running:

- the internal nodes are large-signal time-varying waveforms;
- the transistors are not continuously biased near the switching point;
- \(g_{mn}\) and \(g_{mp}\) are time-varying;
- the inverters are not equivalent to diode-connected devices in series;
- the supply current consists of switching/charging pulses and short-circuit current;
- the “small-signal resistance” becomes a time-periodic or frequency-dependent quantity.

So for the running oscillator, there is no single time-invariant small-signal resistance equal to that expression. A more meaningful low-frequency quantity would be something like

\[
R_{\text{eff}}=\left(\frac{d\langle I_{DD}\rangle}{dV_{DD}}\right)^{-1}
\]

where \(\langle I_{DD}\rangle\) is the average supply current of the oscillating circuit. For an AC supply perturbation, one would need a periodic small-signal/PSS-PAC or harmonic-balance analysis, because supply perturbations can mix with the oscillation frequency.

So the short answer is:

> The formula is valid only for the small-signal linearization about the non-oscillating symmetric bias point. For the actual oscillating circuit, the supply “resistance” is generally different and is not simply \(\frac{1}{3}(1/g_{mn}+1/g_{mp})\).

Let the small-signal input-node voltage be \(v_p\), and define the voltage gain from the input node to the output as

\[
A_v(s)=\frac{v_{out}}{v_p}
\]

including its sign.

Since the MOS gate draws negligible current, the input current flows only into \(C_{in}\) and through \(R_F\). Thus,

\[
i_{in}=sC_{in}v_p+\frac{v_p-v_{out}}{R_F}
\]

\[
i_{in}=v_p\left[sC_{in}+\frac{1-A_v(s)}{R_F}\right]
\]

So the input impedance is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{in}(s)=\frac{v_p}{i_{in}}
=
\frac{1}{sC_{in}+\dfrac{1-A_v(s)}{R_F}}
}
\]

or equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{in}(s)=\frac{R_F}{1-A_v(s)+sR_FC_{in}}
}
\]

If the amplifier is inverting, i.e.

\[
A_v(s)=-A(s),
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
Z_{in}(s)=\frac{R_F}{1+A(s)+sR_FC_{in}}
}
\]

and at low frequency,

\[
\boxed{
R_{in}\approx \frac{R_F}{1+A_0}
}
\]

where \(A_0\) is the midband magnitude of the voltage gain from node \(P\) to \(V_{out}\).

The circuit is a coupled LC quadrature oscillator. Its oscillation frequency is mainly set by the LC tank resonance, but the quadrature-coupling current \(I_1\) shifts it slightly away from the natural tank frequency.

Let

\[
\omega_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{LC_T}}, \qquad 
f_0=\frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC_T}}
\]

where \(C_T\) is the total tank capacitance at each LC node, including MOS parasitics, varactors/load capacitance, etc.

For a tank quality factor \(Q\), and assuming the coupling pair current is \(I_1\) while the main oscillator tail current is \(I_{SS}\), the oscillation frequency is approximately

\[
\boxed{
\frac{\omega_{\text{osc}}}{\omega_0}
\approx
\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{I_1}{2QI_{SS}}\right)^2}
\;\pm\;
\frac{I_1}{2QI_{SS}}
}
\]

or, for weak coupling,

\[
\boxed{
f_{\text{osc}} \approx f_0
\left(1 \pm \frac{I_1}{2QI_{SS}}\right)
}
\]

The sign depends on the coupling polarity/phase sequence. If the coupling is weak or the tank \(Q\) is high, the shift is small, so a practical estimate is simply

\[
\boxed{
f_{\text{osc}} \approx \frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC_T}}
}
\]

Assume small-signal operation, \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are ac grounds, \(I_{SS}\) is ideal, and \(r_o\) is neglected.

Let a test voltage \(v_p\) be applied at node \(P\). For \(M_1\),

\[
v_x=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_p .
\]

The \(M_2\)-\(M_3\) pMOS pair converts \(v_x\) to an output current with effective transconductance

\[
g_{md}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}} .
\]

Hence the open-loop gain from \(P\) to the output is

\[
A_0\equiv \left.\frac{v_{\text{out}}}{v_p}\right|_{R_F\text{ open}}
=-g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{md}R_{D2}.
\]

For matched \(M_2\) and \(M_3\),

\[
g_{md}=\frac{g_{m2}}{2},
\qquad
A_0=-\frac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}.
\]

Including the loading of the output by \(R_F\), the input admittance is

\[
Y_{\text{in}}(s)
=
sC_{\text{in}}
+
\frac{1-A_0}{R_F+R_{D2}}.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=
\left[
sC_{\text{in}}
+
\frac{1+g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{md}R_{D2}}
{R_F+R_{D2}}
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

or, for matched \(M_2\) and \(M_3\),

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{
1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}
+sC_{\text{in}}(R_F+R_{D2})
}
}.
\]

Thus the low-frequency input resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

If \(R_F\gg R_{D2}\), the commonly used approximation is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)\approx
\frac{R_F}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}
+sR_FC_{\text{in}}}
}
\]

and hence

\[
R_{\text{in}}\approx
\frac{R_F}
{1+\dfrac{1}{2}g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}}.
\]

For small signals, \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) are AC grounds. Since both the gate and source of \(M_2\) are fixed, \(M_2\) appears simply as \(r_{o2}\) between node \(X\) and AC ground.

Thus, \(M_1\) has source degeneration \(r_{o2}\), giving

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
=r_{o1}+\left(1+g_{m1}r_{o1}\right)r_{o2}}
\]

where body effect has been neglected. Usually \(g_{m1}r_{o1}\gg 1\), so

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\]

If the body effect of \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\):

\[
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}.
\]

For small signals, \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) are AC grounds. Since both the gate and source of \(M_2\) are fixed, \(M_2\) presents only \(r_{o2}\) from node \(X\) to AC ground. Thus, \(M_1\) is source-degenerated by \(R_S=r_{o2}\).

Neglecting body effect, the output resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
=r_{o1}+\left(1+g_{m1}r_{o1}\right)r_{o2}
}
\]

and, for \(g_{m1}r_{o1}\gg 1\),

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}.}
\]

If the body effect of \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\):

\[
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}.
\]

Assuming both PMOS devices are in saturation, the bias gates and \(V_{DD}\) are small-signal grounds. Thus \(M_2\) has \(v_{gs2}=0\), so it contributes only \(r_{o2}\) from node \(X\) to ac ground. Node \(X\) is therefore the source degeneration node for \(M_1\).

Using a test voltage \(v_t\) at the output,

\[
v_X=i_t r_{o2}
\]

and for \(M_1\),

\[
i_t=\frac{v_t-v_X}{r_{o1}}-(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})v_X .
\]

Solving,

\[
\boxed{
R_{out}=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

If body effect is neglected,

\[
\boxed{
R_{out}=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

Note the boosting term uses \(g_{m1}\), because \(M_1\) is the cascode device whose drain is the output node.

With \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) as small-signal grounds, node \(X\) sees resistance \(r_{o2}\) through \(M_2\). Thus \(M_1\) is source-degenerated by \(r_{o2}\), so

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=
r_{o1}+\left[1+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}\right]r_{o2}
}
\]

equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=
r_{o1}+r_{o2}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}
\approx
(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}
}
\]

If body effect is neglected, set \(g_{mb1}=0\):

\[
R_{\text{out}}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}.
\]

Assume low frequency, negligible gate current, and neglect channel-length modulation/body effect. \(V_{DD}\) and \(V_b\) are ac grounds and \(I_{SS}\) only sets bias.

Node \(X\):

\[
v_X=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_P .
\]

For the source-coupled pair \(M_2,M_3\), with the gate of \(M_3\) at ac ground,

\[
(g_{m2}+g_{m3})v_S=g_{m2}v_X,
\qquad
i_o=g_{m3}v_S
\]

so

\[
i_o=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}v_X .
\]

Thus the open-circuit voltage gain from \(P\) to \(V_{out}\) is

\[
A_v=\frac{v_{out}}{v_P}
=
-g_{m1}R_{D1}
\left(\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}\right)R_{D2}
\equiv -A_0 .
\]

The feedback resistor is reflected to the input as

\[
Z_F=\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1-A_v}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1+A_0}.
\]

Therefore the impedance seen by \(I_{in}\) at node \(P\) is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{in}(s)=
\left[
\frac{1}{R_{in}}
+sC_{in}
+
\frac{
1+g_{m1}R_{D1}
\left(\dfrac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}\right)R_{D2}
}{
R_F+R_{D2}
}
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

Equivalently, \(Z_{in}(s)=R_{eq}/(1+sR_{eq}C_{in})\), where \(R_{eq}\) is the parallel combination above.

If \(R_F\gg R_{D2}\), this reduces to the usual Miller approximation

\[
Z_{in}(s)\approx
\left[
\frac{1}{R_{in}}+sC_{in}+
\frac{1+A_0}{R_F}
\right]^{-1}.
\]
